commit cba9903d39a86dee4bf1f84ff49dbc14b21b33ba
Author: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date:   Fri Aug 28 00:07:51 2026 +0000

    fix(comp): hold an exterior notch open, the way a counter already is
    
    Pull comp grows every shape by one pull per side, and stage 5 has always held
    an INTERIOR ring open when that would shrink it below the sewable floor. The
    same arithmetic closes exterior notches -- an E's arm slots, an N's crotch --
    and nothing asked the question there, because `poly.interiors` is empty for a
    shape whose concavities are notches rather than rings. An O's counters were
    protected; an E's slots were not.
    
    Measured on a block E at 0.3 mm pull: a 0.8 mm slot keeps 0.2 mm. On
    logo_drone_thermal_badge.png the guard now holds 15 pockets spanning
    0.528-0.920 mm, which covers the slots this was reported against (THERMAL's E
    at 0.936 mm, DRONE's at 0.728 mm); the 82 it skips have a median width of
    0.140 mm and are vectorization slivers.
    
    Pockets come from the convex hull of the shape with its holes FILLED. Against
    the raw polygon a hole is absent from the shape and present in its hull, so an
    O's counter returns as a "pocket" and is held twice -- idempotent
    geometrically, but it doubles what the HOLE_NEARLY_CLOSED warning reports.
    Convex hull rather than bounding box because this file already records a defect
    where compensation rode on bounding boxes and "the same artwork sewed
    differently rotated 45 degrees"; a test pins that invariance.
    
    Safe by construction, and stated in the docstring because the caller takes
    `_largest_polygon` afterwards: a pocket is disjoint from the shape, so
    `grown - pocket` still contains all of the original. Holding one can restore an
    outline but can never cut into artwork or split a shape further than it already
    was. An interior hole cannot split a polygon at all; an exterior notch could.
    
    The area floor is kept rather than swapped for a width test, and the boundary
    it produces is documented with the measurement (0.53 mm held at 2.59 mm2,
    0.61 mm skipped at 2.16 mm2) instead of tuned away. Area conflates a slot's
    width with its length; a width criterion would buy a narrow band around 0.6 mm
    at the cost of a new constant, and thresholds calibrated on the wrong
    population are exactly what this defect was.
    
    Five mutations applied and reverted, all killed. A sixth is noted in the
    docstring as unreachable rather than left looking untested: a degenerate
    LineString already fails the caller's area test.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_0169GUJAVhmvoBAywFGpAbUe

diff --git a/digitizer/digitizer_core/stage5_overlap.py b/digitizer/digitizer_core/stage5_overlap.py
index ad1cf52..a638ae8 100644
--- a/digitizer/digitizer_core/stage5_overlap.py
+++ b/digitizer/digitizer_core/stage5_overlap.py
@@ -229,6 +229,37 @@ def _grow(poly, pull: float, axis_deg: float | None):
     return _axial_offset(poly, pull, axis_deg, 1.0)
 
 
+def _exterior_pockets(poly: Polygon) -> list[Polygon]:
+    """The shape's concave pockets — the exterior counterpart of `interiors`.
+
+    Every place the outline turns inward: an E's arm slots, an N's crotch, a
+    C's mouth.
+
+    Differenced against the shape with its holes FILLED, not against the shape
+    itself. `convex_hull - poly` looks right and is not: a hole is absent from
+    `poly` and present in its hull, so an O's counter comes back as a "pocket"
+    and gets held twice — once by the interior loop and once here. Geometrically
+    that is idempotent, but it doubles the count the HOLE_NEARLY_CLOSED warning
+    reports. Filling first leaves the two lists a genuine partition of the
+    negative space the grow can close.
+
+    SAFE BY CONSTRUCTION, and worth stating because the caller subtracts these
+    from a grown shape and then takes `_largest_polygon`. A pocket is disjoint
+    from `poly` by definition, so `grown - pocket` still contains all of
+    `poly`: holding a pocket open can restore the original outline but can
+    never cut into original artwork, and can never split the shape into more
+    pieces than it already had. An interior hole cannot split a polygon at all;
+    an exterior notch could, which is why this needs saying rather than
+    assuming.
+    """
+    filled = Polygon(poly.exterior)
+    pockets = filled.convex_hull.difference(filled)
+    if pockets.is_empty:
+        return []
+    geoms = getattr(pockets, "geoms", [pockets])
+    return [g for g in geoms if g.geom_type == "Polygon" and not g.is_empty]
+
+
 def _shrink(poly, pull: float, axis_deg: float | None):
     """The matching erosion, for asking whether a hole survives compensation."""
     if pull <= 0:
@@ -427,6 +458,35 @@ def resolve_overlaps(
                     continue           # already below the floor; stage 3's call
                 if _shrink(hole, pull, axis).area < hole_floor:
                     held.append(hole)
+            # The same question asked on the OUTSIDE, which the loop above
+            # cannot reach. An E's arm slots and an N's crotch are notches in
+            # the EXTERIOR boundary, not interior rings, so `poly.interiors` is
+            # empty for them — and they close under exactly the same
+            # arithmetic, one pull per side. Measured on a block E with 0.8 mm
+            # slots at 0.3 mm pull: 0.800 mm of slot becomes 0.200 mm, while an
+            # O's counters next to it are protected. That asymmetry is the
+            # defect; the fix is to ask both sides the same question.
+            for pocket in _exterior_pockets(poly):
+                # Same AREA floor the hole loop uses, deliberately, so the two
+                # sides ask one question rather than two. It is not a perfect
+                # measure for a notch: area conflates a slot's width with its
+                # length, so a SHORT wide slot can fall under a floor a LONG
+                # narrow one clears. Measured on `logo_drone_thermal_badge.png`
+                # (2026-08-27) that boundary sits between 0.53 mm held at
+                # 2.59 mm2 and 0.61 mm skipped at 2.16 mm2.
+                #
+                # Left as the area floor anyway. The 15 pockets this holds span
+                # 0.528-0.920 mm, which covers the slots the defect was
+                # reported against (THERMAL's E at 0.936 mm, DRONE's at
+                # 0.728 mm), and the 82 it skips have a MEDIAN width of
+                # 0.140 mm -- vectorization slivers, not artwork. Swapping in a
+                # width criterion would buy a narrow band around 0.6 mm at the
+                # cost of a new tuned constant, and this file already carries
+                # the scars of thresholds calibrated on the wrong population.
+                if pocket.area < hole_floor:
+                    continue           # already below the floor, as above
+                if _shrink(pocket, pull, axis).area < hole_floor:
+                    held.append(pocket)
             if held:
                 holes_held += len(held)
                 grown = _largest_polygon(grown.difference(unary_union(held)))
diff --git a/digitizer/tests/test_pushcomp.py b/digitizer/tests/test_pushcomp.py
